verification.mining_risk:
High for the synthesis, low for each single number. The principle stitches together four incidents from four different programs (etf_forward_rank, bx/beatndx, bestpf_ndx_core, edge round 10). The record holds dozens of programs: mw has 49 angles and about 6,158 variants, nx has 434, edge has 47 families, plus gaps7, moat_*, bestpf with 9.5M combinations, and so on. Proxy checks occur all over the record, and the miner picked the cases where a proxy or conversion distorted something. Counter-cases where proxies were fine are left out:
- synthetic NDX vs QQQ 1999-2026 differed by only 0.3pt/yr;
- the QLD model differed by 0.04pt;
- the EPI proxy overlap gap was -0.24pt.
The 0.25% floor is a single post-hoc threshold. No alternative floors were reported, and that floor itself flips Guns' sign.

High risk of mixing cells. The record has at least these variants:
- two different benchmarks: the castle basket (in practice MSFT) and the whole asset;
- three composites × two windows (short and long);
- all months vs down months;
- correlations rebuilt from holdings (46-67% coverage, using today's holdings for past returns) vs real ETF prices;
- 11 + 6 + 13 candidates in total.

The claim picks the most striking cells across these. GRID's 0.82 is against the whole asset, while VT's 0.82 is against the castle, which is essentially MSFT. The two are put side by side as if they came from the same scale. Some of the record's own caveats were dropped: "VTは判定できない", "benchmarkを取り違えると結論が逆に出る", and the fact that 2243 is a different kind of fund from 2644/200A.

High. The record has attacked "do growing industries pay?" from at least 8 programs: industry_trends (SEC), industry_long, industry_peak, tech_persist, future30 (Q1/Q2/Q3/Q3b plus options), etf_theme (46 ETFs), etf_ai, etf_returns (30), etf_longest (542), net_mix_search and beatndx. The claim keeps the pieces that point the same way and leaves out the ones that do not:
- the SEC version, where momentum industries had positive stock excess (0.245, 7/8);
- SMH ranking 1st over 26 years in the same table;
- software and semis returning 12.5% and 13.8%/yr from 1995 to 2025;
- the 15 of 46 theme ETFs that beat SPY.
The headline examples (TAN, URA, SMH 2000-2010) are the worst cases out of 46 ETFs and the worst start dates. Q1 itself is one pre-registered test with 2 sensitivities, so mining is low there. The mining risk sits in how the principle was assembled.
